# .vercelignore — controls what rides along in the drag-and-drop
# upload to vercel.com/new. Same syntax as .gitignore, applied at
# upload time. Vercel builds fresh from source on their servers, so
# excluding these directories doesn't just save upload bandwidth —
# it also keeps them out of any build cache that could accidentally
# expose local-only artefacts.

# ── Local env / secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────
# .env.local holds SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY, DHAN_TOKEN_ID, and (once
# you set them) APP_PASSWORD + APP_SESSION_SECRET. Uploading it
# would leak the service-role key to Vercel's build logs and — if
# the Vercel project's env vars aren't overriding — into the
# deployed runtime. Set env vars in the Vercel dashboard instead;
# this file is NEVER meant to leave your laptop.
.env.local
.env.*.local

# ── Build artefacts / installed deps ────────────────────────────
# Vercel runs `npm ci` + `next build` on their servers. Anything
# under .next or node_modules would be rebuilt anyway, but leaving
# them in the upload can push a small folder over the 100 MB /
# 15,000-file limits for direct-uploads on the Hobby tier.
node_modules
.next
next-env.d.ts

# ── Editor / OS junk ────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
.vscode
.idea

# ── Migrations + one-off CLI scripts ────────────────────────────
# SQL migrations are applied via Supabase Studio, not the app
# runtime. The scripts/ folder is CLI-only (backfills, verifiers,
# HAR analysers) — Next.js doesn't bundle them because no route
# imports them, but excluding here keeps the deploy artefact
# smaller and gives fewer readable-source breadcrumbs to anyone
# who cracks the login gate.
migrations
scripts

# ── Docs & project notes ────────────────────────────────────────
# No runtime value. Shipping them wastes bytes and exposes design
# intent (attack-surface reconnaissance).
README.md
DEPLOY.md
TODO.md
NOTES.md
docs

# ── HAR / trace files sometimes dumped into the repo during dev
*.har
tracing.zip
